{"items":[{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605823428,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85666,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"<code>Is there a significant correlation between parenting efforts and outcomes</code> How do you measure &quot;outcome&quot; here?  Are you describing IQ tests specifically, or general &quot;success in life&quot; outcomes, or education, or something else?"},{"owner":{"account_id":484995,"reputation":239,"user_id":32787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpOuqhG2.png?s=256","display_name":"MilkLife","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32787/milklife"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605824012,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85667,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe I&#39;d define outcome as the child&#39;s percentile of income as an adult. In other words, can parents reliably ensure that their child gets into the top 10% of all earners in their country? If such research is not available, I&#39;d settle for academic scores or IQ tests."},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user36162"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605824368,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85668,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Jonathan: do you consider giving birth to a child whilst enjoying high socioeconomic status to be a &quot;parenting effort&quot;?"},{"owner":{"account_id":484995,"reputation":239,"user_id":32787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpOuqhG2.png?s=256","display_name":"MilkLife","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32787/milklife"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605824783,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85669,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@dxh no, but presumably well off parents can afford to invest more resources into parenting due to having more money, at least on average"},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605825802,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85671,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Percentile of income?  That&#39;s a very uninteresting question - your income percentile is nearly entirely determined by your parent&#39;s income and background.  Intelligence is not correlated nearly at all with income."},{"owner":{"account_id":484995,"reputation":239,"user_id":32787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpOuqhG2.png?s=256","display_name":"MilkLife","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32787/milklife"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1605826146,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85672,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe people&#39;s kids can be richer or poorer than their parents. For an extreme example see Jeff Bezos."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605826572,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85674,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@JonathanReez Sure, social mobility is not zero.  But by far, the most significant factor in determining your future income level (as a child) is your parent&#39;s socioeconomic status, far more than any childhood intervention would show.  If that&#39;s what you want to see in answers, then be my guest - but it&#39;s not particularly related to &quot;intelligence&quot;, which has very little to do with life outcomes.  Executive function is much more highly correlated with income, for example."},{"owner":{"account_id":484995,"reputation":239,"user_id":32787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpOuqhG2.png?s=256","display_name":"MilkLife","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32787/milklife"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605826675,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85675,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe the answer might just we&#39;ll be &quot;parenting has nothing to do with intelligence/income&quot;, which I&#39;ll accept too if justified by research"},{"owner":{"user_type":"does_not_exist","display_name":"user36162"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605826720,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85676,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Jonathan: but that&#39;s an anecdote, and you&#39;re asking for data! <i>In general</i> socioeconomic status is an incredibly strong predictor to most metrics of success. That will be a massive confounder to control for."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605826895,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85677,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@JonathanReez The problem is that you&#39;re oversimplifying there.  I&#39;m saying that <i>intelligence</i> has relatively little to do with income, not that parenting has nothing to do with intelligence. If that&#39;s the answer, then what - are you going to go back to the other thread and hold this as proof that someone&#39;s wrong? You&#39;d be misusing data, then."},{"owner":{"account_id":484995,"reputation":239,"user_id":32787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/JpOuqhG2.png?s=256","display_name":"MilkLife","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32787/milklife"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605828068,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85680,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe my own parents were quite poor (almost everyone was in the Soviet Union) and I went to a horrible (by US standards) high school that barely had enough funds to paint their walls every year, let alone get quality equipment for students. So I&#39;m biased towards the idea that parents can have a large influence. But that&#39;s just an anecdote. I&#39;m not here to prove anything to that other guy, but rather curious in general."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605828873,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85681,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"That&#39;s fine - then I would edit the question to include your particular criteria, rather than having it in comments."},{"owner":{"account_id":6569274,"reputation":1412,"user_id":17787,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jTz7e.png?s=256","display_name":"chasly - supports Monica","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/17787/chasly-supports-monica"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605834493,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85683,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I made the cited comment. Note that I don&#39;t equate IQ with education. A very loose metaphor would be that IQ is the <i>size</i> of the receptacle and education is what you put into it. An expensively taught student could have a low IQ and someone with barely any education could have a high IQ. Parent input is important."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605841457,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85685,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe - &quot;...income ...is nearly entirely determined by your parent&#39;s income and background.&quot; Wealth is familial, but &quot;income&quot; is much more variable. My family was dirt poor; my ex&#39;s solidly middle class. Our income far exceeded our backgrounds. Most of my (state U) med school classmates were middle class, yet income put them higher. Of my kids (all finished w/ education), one is in the top 5%, while one is in the bottom 25%. We are neither the rule, nor (by far) the exception."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1605841722,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85686,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You&#39;re going to get strong opinions and few verifiable facts, because of the variety of your parameters and the different definition<b>s</b> of intelligence and success. Also, alas, social science (e.g. sociology) is much &quot;softer&quot; than hard science (e.g. physics), affecting how <b>scientific</b> (your emphasis) the answers will be. &quot;can parents reliably ensure that their child gets into the top 10% of all earners in their country?&quot; No. (Opinion!) See my comment above."},{"owner":{"account_id":5711979,"reputation":321,"user_id":26750,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a0bcf85ca259d5c6654c758cf2ee4683?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Kat","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26750/kat"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1605936929,"post_id":40806,"comment_id":85725,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@JonathanReez &quot;can parenting affect intelligence?&quot; is a completely different question than &quot;can parenting affect future income levels?&quot; The answer to each could easily be different. Maybe you&#39;re really asking if parenting can affect a child&#39;s chance of future success? And your other thread is maybe asking how best to do that?"},{"owner":{"account_id":1796213,"reputation":3431,"user_id":21986,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/991a4a6ddb04ff0a0d0ca93849f9b0ac?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"learner101","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21986/learner101"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1606123639,"post_id":40814,"comment_id":85750,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"This is not an answer, because its based on one person&#39;s experience alone (mine). When my son woke up frequently during the night, it was because he was gassy. Here&#39;re all the steps we took to reduce gas during night time:  1. simethicone drops before bed time (for example mylicon) 2. a lot of physical activity during the wake window before bedtime. 3. include massage and cycling legs in night time routine (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jBBPughgJM0\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">youtube.com/watch?v=jBBPughgJM0</a>) 4. Put baby down sleepy but awake (nothing to do with gas, but helps baby learn to go back to sleep on his own if he wakes up)"},{"owner":{"account_id":128901,"reputation":103,"user_id":29880,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/lppfE.png?s=256","display_name":"J...","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/29880/j"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1606145155,"post_id":40815,"comment_id":85752,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I leave a two-step collapsible step-stool there for accessing the top cabinet shelves.  It fills the space, letting it breathe, and keeps cats out... suspect it would do the same for an infant, at least until they get big enough to haul it out of there themselves."},{"owner":{"account_id":15912597,"reputation":101,"user_id":38001,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/42b34d9dcc460757618f2fd035f0c512?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"eps","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38001/eps"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1606160791,"post_id":40815,"comment_id":85761,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It is very common for fridges to be shoved into enclosures with no space on either side.  Yes, you might lose a tiny amount of efficiency but as long as you clean it every couple months for dust buildup you&#39;ll be perfectly fine and way ahead of what most people do (never clean the back)."},{"owner":{"account_id":251740,"reputation":26679,"user_id":316,"user_type":"moderator","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/XDBbz.png?s=256","display_name":"Rory Alsop","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/316/rory-alsop"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1606222802,"post_id":40771,"comment_id":85774,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Please use the chat for discussion about the question. And if you have an answer - please post an answer."},{"owner":{"account_id":5711979,"reputation":321,"user_id":26750,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a0bcf85ca259d5c6654c758cf2ee4683?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Kat","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26750/kat"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1606254295,"post_id":40815,"comment_id":85781,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Two inches doesn&#39;t seem like enough for a baby to get to the back of a fridge. At most they should be able to stick their arm in the gaps on the sides. How big is this gap?"},{"owner":{"account_id":5182392,"reputation":445,"user_id":22177,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/55DUG.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Amazon Dies In Darkness","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/22177/amazon-dies-in-darkness"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1606272650,"post_id":40815,"comment_id":85782,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"That&#39;s where you put the mouse traps.  Babies learn quickly. ;)  Okay, in all seriousness, I used a toilet paper holder.  Screwed it into the wall behind the fridge.  It allowed airflow, but kept the little ones out."},{"owner":{"account_id":1796213,"reputation":3431,"user_id":21986,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/991a4a6ddb04ff0a0d0ca93849f9b0ac?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"learner101","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/21986/learner101"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":5711979,"reputation":321,"user_id":26750,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a0bcf85ca259d5c6654c758cf2ee4683?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Kat","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/26750/kat"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1606283526,"post_id":40815,"comment_id":85783,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Kat 2-3 inches. And yes, all he does is stick his hand in the gap."},{"owner":{"account_id":8014369,"reputation":170,"user_id":33323,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/BpvkV.jpg?s=256","display_name":"AsheraH","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33323/asherah"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1607422589,"post_id":40875,"comment_id":85881,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Have you tried different pacifiers? (Shapes, brands, sizes, ...) Sometimes you need a few tries for it to take."},{"owner":{"account_id":3915121,"reputation":5265,"user_id":6677,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":20,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fOaFT.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jax","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6677/jax"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1607658944,"post_id":40646,"comment_id":85905,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I had a baby who was very happy to play on his own and his dad used to wonder the same thing. Now the same boy practically requires an itinerary to keep him from pestering everyone in the house to play with him, look at his drawings, check out the video he’s watching...don’t worry. Your child will not ignore you forever."},{"owner":{"account_id":10452409,"reputation":235,"user_id":40156,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/196557e98134006cd06dd4363b95cd99?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"eipi","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40156/eipi"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1608499733,"post_id":40814,"comment_id":85943,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Has he recently started solids? A food allergy could be the cause of an upset tummy (&amp;constipation). Try cutting out dairy and see if you have a noticeable improvement. A visit to a physician to check for other issues may also help."},{"owner":{"account_id":1665264,"reputation":2243,"user_id":7778,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":33,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f1edb320793125bf1d36ed27b87b9c51?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"localhost","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7778/localhost"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":10452409,"reputation":235,"user_id":40156,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/196557e98134006cd06dd4363b95cd99?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"eipi","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40156/eipi"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1608502446,"post_id":40814,"comment_id":85944,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@eipi yes he started solids at the of writting and it has been 2 months, one noticeable thing was his 4 front teeth came out together, we thought it might be that but the crying is like a habit now, after every 2 hours, sometimes he will be crying till 1am-2am"},{"owner":{"account_id":1561904,"reputation":101,"user_id":20026,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/PTpfm.jpg?s=256","display_name":"dirkk","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/20026/dirkk"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1608564781,"post_id":40814,"comment_id":85952,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Not directly related to your question, but it should be pointed out: A 8 month old should eat neither sweet nor salty, but just the plain thing (like potatoe, carrots, whatever...)"},{"owner":{"account_id":240600,"reputation":101,"user_id":14697,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/70b13a9ae1aae1ca1aa6ac8dab4643f2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bakuriu","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14697/bakuriu"},"edited":false,"score":45,"creation_date":1610800287,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86280,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"There is zero harm speaking 2 languages with small children. They will automatically learn them both. Children are able to automatically understand that they are 2 different languages, so you don&#39;t need to do anything in particular for &quot;keeping them separate&quot;. Don&#39;t worry about &quot;confusing&quot; the kid. Sure, probably at the beginning they might mix them but in 4-5-6 years they will perfectly speak both."},{"owner":{"account_id":6655813,"reputation":107,"user_id":32337,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fbfvN.jpg?s=256","display_name":"&#198;zor &#198;hai -him-","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32337/%c3%86zor-%c3%86hai-him"},"edited":false,"score":11,"creation_date":1610835212,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86283,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s an important note that bilingual kids <i>do</i> tend to produce fewer words (in one language) than their monolingual peers at younger ages. This isn&#39;t a cause for concern, they rapidly catch up."},{"owner":{"account_id":1516106,"reputation":101,"user_id":40314,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/FZKFx.jpg?s=256","display_name":"James Scott","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40314/james-scott"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1610896116,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86289,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I raised a bilingual son in Spain (other language English) by only speaking English with him as well. In the first 1-3 years be aware that you may not feel like you are making much progress in teaching but rest assured even if not a lot of the second language is being spoken it is all going in. Keep going and push the vocabulary. I left my son with my English only speaking parents to go for dinner for the first time when he was three and we were all worried that he wouldn&#39;t be able to communicate, when I came home my parents told me me he&#39;d spoken great English (for his age) the whole time."},{"owner":{"account_id":1983530,"reputation":103,"user_id":10118,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Yzyhh.jpg?s=256","display_name":"idmean","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/10118/idmean"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":6655813,"reputation":107,"user_id":32337,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fbfvN.jpg?s=256","display_name":"&#198;zor &#198;hai -him-","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32337/%c3%86zor-%c3%86hai-him"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1610914881,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86291,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@AzorAhai-him- Do you have any sources for this claim? This seems to contradictory to the backed claims in the answer below."},{"owner":{"account_id":6655813,"reputation":107,"user_id":32337,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fbfvN.jpg?s=256","display_name":"&#198;zor &#198;hai -him-","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32337/%c3%86zor-%c3%86hai-him"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1983530,"reputation":103,"user_id":10118,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Yzyhh.jpg?s=256","display_name":"idmean","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/10118/idmean"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1610922743,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86293,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@idmean A degree in linguistics. So I don&#39;t remember the citation off the top of my head - but of course everything needs to take in the entire literature, these studies are newer than my degree. Note that there are many, many language pairs (Dutch and French are fairly closely related) and that the second study has between 1-4 <i>individuals</i> per language paired with English. I find that to be a pretty serious drawback of that study."},{"owner":{"account_id":295869,"reputation":103,"user_id":7657,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5925786f174975db18cbc83dd85e5c1d?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paŭlo Ebermann","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7657/pa%c5%adlo-ebermann"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1610931145,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86294,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"9 months seems a bit early for any alphabet knowledge – do not expect too much from it. (Though it certainly does no harm.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":8216,"reputation":343,"user_id":16826,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/R0r8D.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Dan Dascalescu","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16826/dan-dascalescu"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":240600,"reputation":101,"user_id":14697,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/70b13a9ae1aae1ca1aa6ac8dab4643f2?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bakuriu","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/14697/bakuriu"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1610974298,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86299,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Bakuriu: not sure about &quot;zero harm&quot;; there&#39;s an opportunity cost to everything. Instead of Russian, she could learn Chinese, or Spanish. Both <i>may</i> have a <a href=\"https://freakonomics.com/podcast/is-learning-a-foreign-language-really-worth-it-a-new-freakonomics-radio-podcast/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">higher ROI than Russian</a>, modulo personal connections."},{"owner":{"account_id":1280505,"reputation":391,"user_id":38452,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5f418c7981a6e42312b38cebd4f246e0?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Darrel Hoffman","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38452/darrel-hoffman"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1610995392,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86300,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Globally speaking, if you speak 3 languages, you&#39;re average.  2 is below average.  If you speak only 1 language, you&#39;re probably American.  (Note, I am American, though I speak semi-competent French, so, still below average.  Wish I&#39;d been brought up with more languages.)"},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1610999440,"post_id":41053,"comment_id":86302,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"The way this is written, I don&#39;t think it&#39;s a good fit here - it&#39;s just asking for an opinion.  That&#39;s not really a good fit for Stack Exchange sites, including this one.  You could possibly rephrase in a way that could be usefully answered; in particular, the idea is to ask about <i>how to make the decision</i>, both by providing what your end goal is (do you specifically want your child to learn German? Are you thinking it might benefit her beyond the language itself?) and then either asking how to achieve that goal, or asking if you&#39;re right that (end goal is met) by teaching her German."},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1610999541,"post_id":41053,"comment_id":86303,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Right now, we don&#39;t know if you&#39;re asking: &quot;If my end goal is for my daughter to know a second language, can I reasonably achieve that given my partial fluency&quot;, or, &quot;If my end goal is for my daughter to be more well rounded, can I achieve that by teaching her a second language?&quot;, or, &quot;If my end goal is for my daughter to be a better language learner...&quot; etc. - you need to clarify those, and then we can answer those questions - one of them only, please."},{"owner":{"account_id":6655813,"reputation":107,"user_id":32337,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fbfvN.jpg?s=256","display_name":"&#198;zor &#198;hai -him-","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32337/%c3%86zor-%c3%86hai-him"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":8216,"reputation":343,"user_id":16826,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/R0r8D.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Dan Dascalescu","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16826/dan-dascalescu"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1611002016,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86304,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@DanDascalescu That&#39;s stretching the definition of &quot;harm.&quot; And in any case, a Russian-English household can&#39;t just magically teach their kid Chinese."},{"owner":{"account_id":6070507,"reputation":141,"user_id":40326,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Q8m1k.png?s=256","display_name":"Flydog57","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40326/flydog57"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1611017507,"post_id":41034,"comment_id":86305,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"One thing to note. Based on my little experience (my daughter, and a few conversations with others), is that young children that learn two languages this way (one from one source or set of sources, and the other from other sources) do tend to get a little confused when the source/language pairing breaks.  It took years before we could get her to speak English to her grandparents.  When we moved (at 2.5 years old) and daycare went from French to English, we were very surprised when the daycare told us that we needed to work on getting her to speak English - we spoke English at home"},{"owner":{"account_id":1855797,"reputation":150,"user_id":37801,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/54903e0733d46e7c72501b5cc1e62e5b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"The Dude","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37801/the-dude"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1611068642,"post_id":41053,"comment_id":86308,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Joe I&#39;d really like her to learn the language, at least to the level I know it. I can read, write, speak and listen well but I know that I will never be a native speaker -- even if I continue to improve, learn, and practice. Is my partial fluency going to derail the whole thing?"},{"owner":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1611087443,"post_id":41053,"comment_id":86309,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Seemy answer here: <a href=\"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/a/39653/36241\">parenting.stackexchange.com/a/39653/36241</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":1855797,"reputation":150,"user_id":37801,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/54903e0733d46e7c72501b5cc1e62e5b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"The Dude","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37801/the-dude"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1611093563,"post_id":41053,"comment_id":86312,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@SolarMike So what you are saying is, having a decent command of the second language is good enough? How decent is good enough though?"},{"owner":{"account_id":6397988,"reputation":2514,"user_id":36241,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/96f43840d82f2a5c6d5270549422ac6f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Solar Mike","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36241/solar-mike"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1611094783,"post_id":41053,"comment_id":86313,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@TheDude If you read my answer, the last paragraph is apt."},{"owner":{"account_id":19390323,"reputation":461,"user_id":40353,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2e8e4d5f5aa07393d2ca3101898d4873?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"mikem","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40353/mikem"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1611218507,"post_id":40814,"comment_id":86378,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"We have a 9mo and the first thing that came to mind when I read your question was teething.  Even if the teeth are out, they could still be sensitive.  Maybe try some oragel to numb the gums before you put him down and see if it helps."},{"owner":{"account_id":1834330,"reputation":101,"user_id":38125,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/HVb34.jpg?s=256","display_name":"littleO","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38125/littleo"},"edited":false,"score":57,"creation_date":1612288883,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86658,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"One standard piece of advice for raising gifted children is to praise them for their effort and for other qualities they can control, rather than for their intelligence which they can&#39;t control. The idea is that if they are praised for their intelligence, then doing intellectually difficult things becomes scary, because if they struggle it hurts their self-image. This essay <a href=\"https://terrytao.wordpress.com/career-advice/advice-on-gifted-education/\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">Advice on Gifted Education</a> by Terence Tao is interesting."},{"owner":{"account_id":1085259,"reputation":101,"user_id":9347,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Fp4Pm.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Dawood ibn Kareem","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9347/dawood-ibn-kareem"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612288884,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86659,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I had &quot;teach a tot&quot; at that age.  Brilliant.  Because I could form words, before I had the manual dexterity to work a pencil."},{"owner":{"account_id":420593,"reputation":446,"user_id":17680,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/kOkzL.png?s=256","display_name":"WBT","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/17680/wbt"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1612298261,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86667,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;had learned ~25 car brands and said their names when they drove by...&quot; I can&#39;t do that and I spend an above-average amount of time around people who design them. Anyway, check out Mensa&#39;s programs for gifted &amp; talented youth; they have some resources."},{"owner":{"account_id":11007,"reputation":119,"user_id":19783,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2cd7a11b6aa86a60a4cdd40755e41e4b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Roman Odaisky","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/19783/roman-odaisky"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1834330,"reputation":101,"user_id":38125,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/HVb34.jpg?s=256","display_name":"littleO","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38125/littleo"},"edited":false,"score":9,"creation_date":1612304824,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86670,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@littleO Beware the “praise their effort” approach. It seems successful as in it successfully entices children to work more, but there’s a huge confounder: children are well aware that sometimes it’s the result that matters, and sometimes it’s the appearance of making an effort; and often, what the teachers praise simply tells the children <a href=\"https://www.lesswrong.com/posts/NMoLJuDJEms7Ku9XS/guessing-the-teacher-s-password\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">their password</a>. Did you motivate them to work more or did you simply switch them into the other mode?"},{"owner":{"account_id":2494278,"reputation":101,"user_id":6415,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/3GyKiKMl.jpg?s=256","display_name":"mirabilos","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6415/mirabilos"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1612307173,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86672,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It’s not normal, but I at 3 sat happily in the corner reading in Kindergarten instead of playing with the other children. My parents just let me…"},{"owner":{"account_id":5815521,"reputation":251,"user_id":28820,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/fd61af6765e21930006cbbe4b2c6d617?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"alephzero","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/28820/alephzero"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612310637,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86675,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Personal anecdote: I certainly wasn&#39;t <i>taught</i> to read by anybody, but starting school at age 5 I was quickly sent off to the second year reading classes instead of being bored to death with learning the alphabet. For some reason I remember being confused at that age by knowing what a full stop was, but not understanding why &quot;Mr. Frog&quot; was not the end of one sentence and the start of another one. And having learned to read text at 5, I taught myself how to read music notation at age 6 or 7. (And I don&#39;t think I am particularly &quot;gifted&quot; either)."},{"owner":{"account_id":12267596,"reputation":101,"user_id":37951,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/69ba6f53aec2c74129c8499e3532d3ef?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Issel","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/37951/issel"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1612320373,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86680,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Knowledge will come, raise your child to be understanding, kind, empathetic, and to know right from wrong. There are geniuses who are completely useless because they lack these important qualities."},{"owner":{"account_id":6085540,"reputation":177,"user_id":16159,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Haz6W.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Glorfindel","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16159/glorfindel"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612346878,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86685,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Sounds like me; be careful, or he ends up spending too much time on Stack Exchange thirty years later..."},{"owner":{"account_id":1280505,"reputation":391,"user_id":38452,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5f418c7981a6e42312b38cebd4f246e0?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Darrel Hoffman","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38452/darrel-hoffman"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1612362151,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86689,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I wouldn&#39;t be surprised if this story becomes very common for the current generation of children.  Having full access to parents all day will likely result in a net positive.  Someone will need to do a comparative study on this generation of children raised during quarantine compared to those before and after to see how much of an effect this had on early development."},{"owner":{"account_id":12984,"reputation":169,"user_id":414,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/34d142b034e7b194fc1708f3b20d9568?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Mark Rogers","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/414/mark-rogers"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1612367398,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86691,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"My toddler is slightly ahead of these milestones, so I doubt its totally abnormal.  However, natural intelligence doesn&#39;t always translate into functional intelligence.  We are having trouble getting him motivated for potty training, because he already wanted to do it at one point, but now doesn&#39;t care.  <b>Most child prodigies live mediocre lives.</b>  I think that&#39;s partly because knowing they are smart means they don&#39;t feel the need to prove themselves.  Their early intelligence means they are effectively less intelligent later, because intellectual pursuits are less interesting to them."},{"owner":{"account_id":680,"reputation":1503,"user_id":9114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/747ffa5da3538e66840ebc0548b8fd58?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Greg Hewgill","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9114/greg-hewgill"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1612400067,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":86700,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Just as a reference point, have you read any material about &quot;strong willed child&quot;? It sounds like your first child has raised your expectations and your second child is quite different. Different children will need different techniques. You say you&#39;ve &quot;tried all techniques I&#39;ve read&quot;, but we don&#39;t know what you&#39;ve read."},{"owner":{"account_id":20625205,"reputation":71,"user_id":40552,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/b4e30985026dd7454af031ec615ecd02?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Anondad","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40552/anondad"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":680,"reputation":1503,"user_id":9114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/747ffa5da3538e66840ebc0548b8fd58?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Greg Hewgill","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9114/greg-hewgill"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612400172,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":86701,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@GregHewgill I don&#39;t remember that in particular. Maybe a link would help me."},{"owner":{"account_id":680,"reputation":1503,"user_id":9114,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/747ffa5da3538e66840ebc0548b8fd58?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Greg Hewgill","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9114/greg-hewgill"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612400347,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":86702,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I don&#39;t mean to be flippant, but try this, there&#39;s a ton of material out there, since this is not an uncommon situation: <a href=\"https://duckduckgo.com/?q=strong+willed+child\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">duckduckgo.com/?q=strong+willed+child</a>"},{"owner":{"account_id":3915121,"reputation":5265,"user_id":6677,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":20,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fOaFT.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jax","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6677/jax"},"edited":false,"score":6,"creation_date":1612402446,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":86703,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"You use the word “hate” but say you don’t wish the child harm. I don’t think you actually <i>hate</i> the kid. The words that come to mind, from your description, are resentment, disappointment,discontent, regret. These are just as unsettling, and, unwelcome I’m sure. It’s not talked about openly, but I bet many parents have at one time or another felt these same feelings. Maybe not all at once...but parenting is ^HARD^ It took a lot of courage for you to be honest with yourself about this and to seek help, which puts you at risk to be judged. Shining a light on these dark feelings..."},{"owner":{"account_id":3915121,"reputation":5265,"user_id":6677,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":20,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/fOaFT.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Jax","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6677/jax"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1612402749,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":86704,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"...is the first step towards rooting them out."},{"owner":{"account_id":20533619,"reputation":639,"user_id":40416,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/-tMDfgajeT6g/AAAAAAAAAAI/AAAAAAAAAAA/AMZuucncIwFd0iuLXEgtcMoyIuC0okjSZA/s96-c/s256-rj/photo.jpg","display_name":"Bahar Ayka&#231;","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40416/bahar-ayka%c3%a7"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1612421488,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":86711,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I think you are being hard on yourself. If it was real hate hate, you would never bother to write here. When it comes to topic, how were you when you were a kid? Were you obedient? A nice child?  Maybe your second child triggers something about it."},{"owner":{"account_id":48638,"reputation":127,"user_id":38213,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/87af410c7986beb8583c1015265b64d1?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Sebastiaan van den Broek","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38213/sebastiaan-van-den-broek"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1834330,"reputation":101,"user_id":38125,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/HVb34.jpg?s=256","display_name":"littleO","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/38125/littleo"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612437374,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86717,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@littleO as a former gifted kid, this really hit home for me. On top of what you mentioned, I started hiding failures and also didn’t learn to actually work for something because I didn’t understand that was also needed."},{"owner":{"account_id":1554324,"reputation":357,"user_id":27621,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a7c19e1d6860e0e908fe3bdd165f3eb9?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"RonJohn","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/27621/ronjohn"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612457479,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86721,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;Is it normal for a child ...&quot; Do they still publish the <i>What To Expect When...</i> books?"},{"owner":{"account_id":27701,"reputation":101,"user_id":1040,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/Hsume.png?s=256","display_name":"Rob P.","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/1040/rob-p"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612514154,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86725,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Generally, when we talk about developmental norms, there is an assumption of some &#39;normal amount of effort&#39;. We assume differences in ability are the result of differences in aptitude or intelligence, not effort. As a former &#39;gifted&#39; child with parents that pushed early education, there is a huge difference between a child who is casually exposed to counting or reading and quickly/naturally demonstrates an aptitude and a child who follows a counting or reading regimen earlier than their peers, who demonstrates a normal aptitude for it."},{"owner":{"account_id":15284488,"reputation":291,"user_id":36356,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/238a4bb1241086158ec0cd2fe10250ad?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Allerleirauh","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36356/allerleirauh"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612557275,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":86738,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Did you tried to find a good reason for the child to stand up in the morning? (The answer should help me, to understand the ways of arguing you tried)"},{"owner":{"account_id":7813880,"reputation":7904,"user_id":30366,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f996f3ea5fc3e9daa68d0f98db62b6be?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Meg","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30366/meg"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612559172,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":86740,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"My first child is much like your second, he was never an easy baby, fights every bedtime and does not sleep well at night (and likes to lie in only on mornings when I have an early meeting, never on weekends), has his own opinion about most everything.  Yet I am delighted in him.  He&#39;s hilarious, makes up the best and silliest games, gives great hugs, he loves learning things.  Being stubborn and a challenge to live with at 3 is more normal than not for children,  Maybe it would help to think on what the BEST things about your child are and see how you can nurture those qualities,"},{"owner":{"account_id":7813880,"reputation":7904,"user_id":30366,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/f996f3ea5fc3e9daa68d0f98db62b6be?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Meg","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30366/meg"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612559586,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86741,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;m one of those who read at age three, and grew into a perfectly ordinary adult.  I did do well in school, but certainly not the next Steven Hawking or anything of that sort.  I also experienced the fact that while I could -read all he words- of challenging books at quite a young age, I was not at all emotionally mature enough to understand the true meaning of, for example, A Tale of Two Cities, until many years later.  Different skills and intelligences develop in different rates for each person,"},{"owner":{"account_id":4520591,"reputation":138,"user_id":40304,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/a3ffce62783f4b8763a6878d9a57ab72?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Aravind Suresh Thakidayil","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40304/aravind-suresh-thakidayil"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1612861403,"post_id":41187,"comment_id":86762,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I could read at 2 and a half years and could write and speak two languages at four. I&#39;m not bloody smart, no. Some kids learn early, that&#39;s all."},{"owner":{"account_id":350246,"reputation":145,"user_id":29120,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/5620010c3d18b26c8a0ea6b16218fa9b?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"crobar","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/29120/crobar"},"edited":false,"score":8,"creation_date":1613397965,"post_id":41259,"comment_id":86790,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Baby can arrive with a present for toddler, toddler can also bring present for baby."},{"owner":{"account_id":141314,"reputation":201,"user_id":9896,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/7e361831338d5d1b5df96974fe7a172f?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Francis Davey","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9896/francis-davey"},"edited":false,"score":3,"creation_date":1613428000,"post_id":41259,"comment_id":86798,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Mum had me feel for my little sister kicking and generally got me really hyped about having a baby sister."},{"owner":{"account_id":15284488,"reputation":291,"user_id":36356,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/238a4bb1241086158ec0cd2fe10250ad?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Allerleirauh","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/36356/allerleirauh"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1613626531,"post_id":41275,"comment_id":86870,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"We used the rulemaker as difference (our son was older, but maybe a younger one would understand this too). We said something along the line &quot;This is aunt&#39;s bookshelf, and she do not want to it to be climbed&quot; So he learned, to ask for permission if he do not knows the rules yet (different to households we visit regularly)"},{"owner":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1613754724,"post_id":41305,"comment_id":86930,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Kealeigh, welcome to the site.  I made some edits to clean up the grammar, and I also added an actual question - if this isn&#39;t what you&#39;re asking, please clarify.  But be aware, we&#39;re not able to answer medical questions, so if the question is &quot;should I take my child to the doctor&quot;, the answer is &quot;yes&quot;, but not because of anything we&#39;re able to say about your situation - that&#39;s the answer any time you have to ask.  :)"},{"owner":{"account_id":2489521,"reputation":181,"user_id":40380,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/5gy1r.png?s=256","display_name":"Jack M.","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40380/jack-m"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1613761303,"post_id":41305,"comment_id":86933,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"A doctor should be your first step, would be bad to suggest something thinking the issue is one thing and then have the issue be something else and harm the child with one of the many suggestions I have."},{"owner":{"account_id":939235,"reputation":5323,"user_id":33055,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/zOso6.jpg?s=256","display_name":"Timur Shtatland","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/33055/timur-shtatland"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1613767644,"post_id":41305,"comment_id":86937,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"What kind of solid foods have you offered? Please give some specific examples."},{"owner":{"account_id":190624,"reputation":2544,"user_id":31496,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/0eb07ddb42ad7121a9b57508dd0b89ce?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Bart van Ingen Schenau","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/31496/bart-van-ingen-schenau"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1614249924,"post_id":41305,"comment_id":86983,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Does your child make any attempt to chew their food if you present them with something that can&#39;t be swallowed directly?"},{"owner":{"account_id":11998458,"reputation":979,"user_id":30102,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/765dced4f68700e37c91bf8be68d0bcc?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"nick012000","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/30102/nick012000"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1614638769,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":87027,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Have you considered taking the child to a psychologist? It&#39;s possible that they might have a condition like oppositional defiant disorder."},{"owner":{"account_id":1078902,"reputation":101,"user_id":3474,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/qyuv4.png?s=256","display_name":"Chimera","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/3474/chimera"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1616018222,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":87185,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"It&#39;s interesting that you call your second child &quot;it&quot;. That might be part of the problem right there."},{"owner":{"account_id":10452409,"reputation":235,"user_id":40156,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/196557e98134006cd06dd4363b95cd99?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"eipi","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40156/eipi"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1078902,"reputation":101,"user_id":3474,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/qyuv4.png?s=256","display_name":"Chimera","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/3474/chimera"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1616097039,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":87199,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@Chimera it may be a language issue. Some second-language English speakers may use &quot;it&quot; mistakenly to remain gender neutral when &quot;they&quot; is more appropriate."},{"owner":{"account_id":2443084,"reputation":492,"user_id":16570,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/bcc10f0e4b08d0d5927f07e62456768a?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"DRF","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/16570/drf"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":10452409,"reputation":235,"user_id":40156,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/196557e98134006cd06dd4363b95cd99?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"eipi","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/40156/eipi"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1619700888,"post_id":41213,"comment_id":88442,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@eipi it&#39;s worse than that. Some might use it even when not trying to remain gender neutral just because in their own language &quot;it&quot; is acceptable or even appropriate for a child even if the gender is known. In Czech the correct pronoun for a child is &quot;it&quot; (though if I talk about my daughter I&#39;d use she, it&#39;s a bit complicated) and since we conjugate verbs differently based on gender (we have three masculine/feminine/neutral, you try being gender neutral in Czech.:D) you need to use the neutral verb conjugation. This can make it pretty confusing when talking about a child in a second language."},{"owner":{"account_id":920526,"reputation":1885,"user_id":32290,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vPzRc.png?s=256","display_name":"Flater","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32290/flater"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1629720817,"post_id":41305,"comment_id":89229,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"When you say &#39;throw up&#39;, do you mean vomiting, or willfully spitting it out?"},{"owner":{"account_id":420593,"reputation":446,"user_id":17680,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/kOkzL.png?s=256","display_name":"WBT","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/17680/wbt"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1644945025,"post_id":40226,"comment_id":90507,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Consider doing this just before the diaper change, rather than just after; bladder control may not really be there yet."},{"owner":{"account_id":3674367,"reputation":72549,"user_id":9327,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/jlp4Q.png?s=256","display_name":"anongoodnurse","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9327/anongoodnurse"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1658852008,"post_id":40226,"comment_id":91556,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"&quot;We sometimes make our 7 months old baby laugh...&quot; How do you do that? Peek-a-boo? Funny faces? With toys? Tickling? It matters. Thanks."},{"owner":{"account_id":920526,"reputation":1885,"user_id":32290,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/vPzRc.png?s=256","display_name":"Flater","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/32290/flater"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1659529923,"post_id":40226,"comment_id":91591,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"A bit of a reframe: laughing, as opposed to <i>what</i>? Crying? Not engaging your child? Avoiding emotion altogether? Doing things they don&#39;t like? Depending on what it is you&#39;re trying to home in on, you&#39;re going to get a different answer."},{"owner":{"account_id":26518172,"reputation":405,"user_id":43605,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/915fbda190bd0c15ac7e79de80bc0899?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Steve V","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/43605/steve-v"},"edited":false,"score":1,"creation_date":1666885582,"post_id":39854,"comment_id":91971,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"To be clear, are you asking about a sleeping cradle for use when the baby is sleeping, or a cradle for use when the baby is awake?  There are markedly different implications."},{"owner":{"account_id":1968759,"reputation":3144,"user_id":13650,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/723ef9d059b1582d42f1f3bd85326cd3?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG&f=y&so-version=2","display_name":"Cdn_Dev","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13650/cdn-dev"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1680805861,"post_id":39607,"comment_id":92768,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@deworde you have to understand that this question was coming from someone with no concept of parenting whatsoever. It&#39;s not about crying being problematic, it&#39;s about understanding night feeds."},{"owner":{"account_id":20553,"reputation":10800,"user_id":7798,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/431cd43aaf713caa80316e852a87a840?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Paul Johnson","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/7798/paul-johnson"},"edited":false,"score":2,"creation_date":1683020500,"post_id":40435,"comment_id":92838,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"One other concern I&#39;d have is legal custody frameworks. There have been cases where a co-parent has moved to another country where the custody rules are more friendly to them and then refused to let the child come back. So it depends on how much the mother trusts the father on this."},{"owner":{"account_id":482697,"reputation":4291,"user_id":13387,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/efdd0371ee7b983ab3b858ea92653aeb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"dsollen","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13387/dsollen"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1691673185,"post_id":40226,"comment_id":93400,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"I&#39;m afraid I don&#39;t have the specific studies.  However from an evolutionary standpoint we know both laughter and smiles evolved as a key part of parent-child interaction.  This is the way babies that can&#39;t yet communicate express their approval of an activity.  Laughter decreases as one ages, and this is largely because it&#39;s need is superseded by language and direct communication once verbal skills develop sufficiently.  In short evolution itself is encouraging laughter as a form of communication meaning it&#39;s unlikely to be harmful."},{"owner":{"account_id":482697,"reputation":4291,"user_id":13387,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":50,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/efdd0371ee7b983ab3b858ea92653aeb?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"dsollen","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/13387/dsollen"},"reply_to_user":{"account_id":1780022,"reputation":63542,"user_id":6524,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":80,"profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/40931bddde219f1a9e4514d2f4cfa826?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"Joe","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/6524/joe"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1691673300,"post_id":40226,"comment_id":93401,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@joe tickling is indeed unique as it&#39;s the one form of laughter that may not be good and adults should make clear to children they have the right to &#39;opt out&#39; of tickling and they will be respected.  Going back to my evolutionary discussion though tickling evolved as a mean to train children to protect vulnerable parts of their body from attack.  They laugh to encourage adults to keep up the &#39;training&#39;, but they may not enjoy it since their suppose to be learning how to protect against tickling and thus defend these body parts."},{"owner":{"account_id":8049,"reputation":10334,"user_id":147,"user_type":"registered","accept_rate":100,"profile_image":"https://i.sstatic.net/UNO2C.jpg?s=256","display_name":"deworde","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/147/deworde"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1691845438,"post_id":39607,"comment_id":93436,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"@mcraenich It&#39;s not a criticism of the poster, it&#39;s just my favourite common problem on the site."},{"owner":{"account_id":1312213,"reputation":4224,"user_id":9368,"user_type":"registered","profile_image":"https://www.gravatar.com/avatar/2442ea911c89187262d6575d739a1ca7?s=256&d=identicon&r=PG","display_name":"swbarnes2","link":"https://parenting.stackexchange.com/users/9368/swbarnes2"},"edited":false,"score":0,"creation_date":1706728061,"post_id":39499,"comment_id":94224,"content_license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","body":"Have you asked your son if he desires this kind of contact?  If he has moved past it, but mother is insisting, it will be easier for you to argue that it should stop."}],"has_more":false,"quota_max":300,"quota_remaining":274}